  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Shelter for Populations Affected by the Cyclone in the Great South-East of Madagascar</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Madagascar is highly exposed to natural hazards such as cyclones, floods, and drought because of its location in the southwestern Indian Ocean basin. Dependence on rain-fed agriculture, poor water availability, and inadequate infrastructure, exacerbated by poverty, have reduced resilience to natural hazards. On 21 February 2023, Tropical Cyclone Freddy made its first landfall in Madagascar, which has affected 96,560 people, led to significant infrastructure damage and an increase in the number of internally displaced persons.The International Organization for Migration (IOM) and its partners are responding to the most urgent humanitarian needs of those affected by Freddy in 31 municipalities. IOM is contributing to the restoration of dignified living conditions for the most vulnerable 6,714 households (30,214 people), specifically through the implementation of a &#x201c;cash for shelter&#x201d; programme through partners and the rehabilitation of three community halls.</narrative>
  </description>
